me other the DEA  had been in the dealership asking  questions and I feel I thought it was  sort of ludicrous I didn't think I would  be indicted for that this is something  that would blow over and you know I'll  be cleared but that was the farthest  thing from the truth  so my husband was 11 years older and he  was a heroin addict he was involved with  some friends of his that would  manufacture meth and they would sell the  meth and then they would buy heroin and  they would get high in party they jury  found me guilty of conspiracy and I  guess in their eyes they believed that I  knew what was going on no matter if I  knew what was going on if I wasn't no  part of it then it didn't have anything  to do with me so being found guilty of  conspiracy that charge makes you liable  for every single thing that anybody ever  did you kind of think what is this a  mistake you know this is here you're  kidding here and I remember at  sentencing when they brought me in for  it then the judge took a break he said  excuse me we have to take care of  something and then he brought me in and  you know they the lawyers all remain  seated and he proceeded in sentencing me  and after he slapped a gavel and he left  you know all the other lawyers got up  and walked over to where we were and  said hey that's ridiculous he can't do  that and so I had that in my head that  you know you know this was wrong they  had said something in the papers cuz I  was all over the papers I was the  headline I was the black part of my beer  dealer which was something unusual  specially female in the eighties so the  news had said from 30 years to life and  all kind of stuff they threw out I  didn't actually know until I got to  court  day that I would receive 188 months in  prison and I had no idea of what any of  that meant until I got to prison and  started and that's when it really sunk  in as to the severity of the sentence  during the trial my attorney was telling  me they can sentence you to 30 years in  prison and I still it did not compute  even when I was finally sentenced to 30  years I still didn't believe it I'm  still in my mind thinking this is not  happening there's no way I'm doing 30  years in prison it makes no sense as  governor michael dukakis vetoed  mandatory sentences for drug dealers  he vetoed the death penalty everybody  wants to be tough on crime and that was  the mantra the injured to be tough on  crime presidential elections were lost  because people say they weren't tough  enough on crime I was not a gangster and  most of the people that I worked with  never carried any guns we didn't rely on  any kind of violence or threats and it  was just too much money you know the  opportunity was there and when I say  money I mean Millions  it finally really hit me that um yeah  I'm gonna do 30 years in prison and no I  do not want to do 30 years in prison  there's no way  I was in FCI Tallahassee at the time and  they have these really tall buildings  and I got on the roof of one of the  buildings and I took a razor blade and I  cut my throat and I jumped off and they  came and they took me to the hospital I  was DOA on arrival and I spent four  months in the hospital pretty much with  everything broken I had like seven  surgeries to repair everything they  thought I would be paralyzed I'd learn  how to walk all over again and well and  I was in the hospital in those four  months realizing okay well I I did pull  through this and now I might be  paralyzed spending thirty years in  prison that's not even that doesn't make  the situation better I realized you know  I'm gonna have to try to figure out the  best way to you know get through this  because this is a reality and I am gonna  spend thirty years in prison and you  know how am I gonna deal with this once  you exhaust your appeals that you've  been in courts don't want to hear from  you anymore even if they've made the  mistake they don't care they don't want  you back but the federal go